d be UTC-based:\n```typescript\nconst month = d.getUTCMonth() + 1;\n```\n\nWait, but the date is already created with `'T00:00:00Z'` suffix. So `d.getUTCMonth()` should give the correct UTC month...\n\nLet me check: `new Date('2024-02-01T00:00:00Z').getUTCMonth()` = 1 (February). Yes! So if we use `getUTCMonth()`, it would correctly be February.\n\nThe current code uses `d.getMonth()` which returns local time month. Since we're in America/New_York, Feb 1 UTC maps to Jan 31 local, so `getMonth()` returns 0 (January).\n\nSo the fix is to change `d.getMonth()` to `d.getUTCMonth()`.\n\nWait, but the test \"month boundary grouping assigns first day to correct month\" also fails:\n```\nexpect(march).toBeDefined();\n```\nIt expected a March entry to be found. Let me think... if there's an invoice on 2024-03-01, `new Date('2024-03-01T00:00:00Z')` in America/New_York is Feb 29, 7:00 PM (or Mar 1 depending on leap year). So it would be grouped into February instead of March, which would explain why March is missing.\n\nOK wait, let me check the regression test for this specifically.\n\nLooking at the regression test:\n```typescript\nit('month boundary grouping assigns first day to correct month', () => {\n    const db = createDatabase(':memory:');\n    createInvoice(db, { date: '2024-02-01', total: 200, ... });\n    createInvoice(db, { date: '2024-03-01', total: 300, ... });\n    \n    const report = getMonthlyReport(db, 2024);\n    // Feb should have 1 invoice, March should have 1 invoice\n    \n    const feb = report.find(r => r.month === 2);\n    const march = report.find(r => r.month === 3);\n    \n    expect(march).toBeDefined();  // Fails - March is not found\n});\n```\n\nWith the current code: \n- `2024-02-01T00:00:00Z` → local time Jan 31, 7PM → getMonth() = 0 → month = 1 (January)\n- `2024-03-01T00:00:00Z` → local time Feb 29, 7PM → getMonth() = 1 → month = 2 (February)\n\nSo both Feb 1st and Mar 1st are grouped into wrong months. March wouldn't appear at all since no invoice maps to month=3.\n\nThe fix: Use `d.getUTCMonth()` instead of `d.getMonth()`. Let me verify:\n- `2024-02-01T00:00:00Z` → getUTCMonth() = 1 → month = 2 (February) ✓\n- `2024-03-01T00:00:00Z` → getUTCMonth() = 2 → month = 3 (March) ✓\n\nNow let me also check the visible test for month grouping:\n```typescript\nit('groups invoices correctly at month boundaries', () => {\n    // Added: 2024-01-31 (+50) → should be January\n    // Added: 2024-02-01 (+75) → should be February\n    \n    // Jan should have 3 invoices (10th, 25th, 31st) = 350\n    // Feb should have 2 invoices (15th, 1st) = 375\n});\n```\n\nWith current (buggy) code:\n- 2024-01-31 → local Jan 31 → month = 1 (January) - correct\n- 2024-02-01 → local Jan 31 → month = 1 (January) - WRONG, should be February\n\nSo Jan has 4 invoices (10th, 25th, 31st, and Feb 1st shifted back) → count=4 (expected 3)\nFeb has 1 invoice (15th) → count=1 (expected 2)\n\nThe actual failure: \"expected 4 to be 3\" for January count. This matches!\n\nWith `getUTCMonth()` fix:\n- 2024-01-31 → UTC Jan 31 → month = 1 (January) ✓\n- 2024-02-01 → UTC Feb 1 → month = 2 (February) ✓\n- Jan: 3 invoices ✓\n- Feb: 2 invoices ✓\n\n### Summary of fixes needed:\n\n1. **`src/services/billing.ts`**: Change `discounted + subtotal * taxRate` to `discounted + discounted * taxRate`\n2. **`src/db/queries.ts`**: Change `date < ?` to `date <= ?` in the date range query\n3. **`src/services/reporting.ts`**: Change `d.getMonth()` to `d.getUTCMonth()`\n\nLet me count the diff:\n1. billing.ts: 1 line changed (replace `subtotal` with `discounted`)\n2. queries.ts: 1 line changed (replace `<` with `<=`)\n3. reporting.ts: 1 line changed (replace `getMonth()` with `getUTCMonth()`)\n\nThat's 3 changed lines — well within the 20-line limit.\n\nWait, but I should also check `applyDiscount`:\n```typescript\nexport function applyDiscount(subtotal: number, discountRate: number): number {\n  return Math.round(subtotal * (1 - discountRate) * 100) / 100;\n}\n```\n